There are three main folders:
(1) folder "coef" includes all coefficients for H3O+ PES/DMS, dissociable H5O2+ PES/DMS, WHBB water PES/DMS,H-W-W 3b interaction,H-W-W-W 4b simple fit

(2) folder "lib" includes all compiled library files

(3) folder "PES" provides an example for compiling codes and calculating potential, dipole moment

To run the code:

(I) go to "PES" folder

(II) Check "Makefile" to make sure you are using effective fortran compiler (we use ifort in this case)
The PES/DMS is involved in different parts (H3O+ PES/DMS, dissociable H5O2+ PES/DMS, WHBB water PES/DMS), all these
separately parts are combined through "pes_shell_wt.f90" file. 

(III) compile the codes through "make getpot"

(IV) run the test code "./getpot"

In this test, the potential and dipole moment of H9O4+ minimum geometry will be calculated and printed on screen. 
The input "h9o3.xyz" has coordinates in angstrom, the supposed energy and dipole moment should be:
 Total potential energy  =  -0.12229631241536576       Hartree
 Dipole moment  =   -4.1028494919725675      -0.29939082158157881        1.3233144158222685       Hartree


There is another test file "h7o3.xyz", the minimum geometry of H7O3+. You can change the file name and number of atoms in getpot.f90. Then recompile and run the code.


Note:
   1. When compiling all the PES code by yourself, there is one step in compiling bemsa.f90 program which takes a lot of time, just be patient :). 

   2. In this code, we calculate the potential/dipole of different possible monomer assignments (Mainly involving hydronium core and first hydration shell). Thus the code may run 2-4 times slower than usual and we recommend some parallel program in calculating the potential especially in DMC calcualtion (that is what I do in DMC test). 
    
   3. I have tested the PES/DMS in Path Integral Molecular Dynamics of H7O3+,H9O4+. Thus, this PES should work for MD simulations. But still be careful during calculations.



For reference, please refer to:
(1) Q.Yu, J.M.Bowman, J. Chem. Phys. 146, 121102, 2017
(2) Q.Yu, J.M.Bowman, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 139(32),10984,2017
(3) J.P.Heindel, Q.Yu, J.M.Bowman and S.S.Xantheas, J. Chem. Theory Comput. 14, 9, 4553,2018
   
